--TEST--
A property with nothing to fill it, a path out of a constructor that leaves one unassigned, a default that has to be computed and a constant with no type are each refused where they are written — `rule:classes/definite-property-initialization`, `rule:types/declaration`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/properties-and-constants
// Four ways a slot could be read before it holds anything, and each is
// refused at its cause rather than at the read. `E0409` is
// `rule:classes/definite-property-initialization` twice over — once where
// there is no constructor to attach the obligation to, and once where there
// is one and a path out of it assigns nothing — so a class cannot reach the
// run at all with a slot that was never filled.
//
// The other two are about *when* the value exists: a default is written into
// every fresh instance before any code runs, so it has to be known by then,
// and a constant is read at its declared type, so the type is written where
// every other one is.
class Child extends Base {
    public string $own;
}

class Base {
    public int $kept = 1;
}

class Limits {
    public const WIDTH = 5;
    public int $n;
    public string $computed = Core\Str::upper("x");

    public function constructor(bool $lucky) {
        if ($lucky) {
            $this->n = 1;
        }
    }
}

echo "never reached\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0409]: `$own` has no default value, and `Child` declares no constructor to assign it
%A
error[E0246]: a constant declares its type
%A
error[E0409]: `$n` is not assigned on every path out of the constructor
%A
error[E0472]: a property default must be a `string` constant
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
